---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="ToolkitRC P200 Reverse Engineering — CopperTrace"
  description="ToolkitRC P200 browser control through a Pico: adjustable voltage/current, verified ON/OFF, live measurements and USB-C OFF/AUTO in Labrax Bench Control."
>
  <article class="article">
    <div class="article-meta">Project 001 · Updated 14 October 2026 · Working bench prototype</div>
    <h1>Reverse engineering the ToolkitRC P200</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      We can now set voltage and current limit, switch the main output and read measurements
      from a browser. A Pico USB bridge and a custom firmware extension connect a working
      P200 V1 to Labrax Bench Control, alongside USB-C OFF/AUTO controls.
    </p>

    <div class="callout success">
      <strong>Verified on the bench.</strong>
      The main output delivered 5 V and 6 V, APPLY changed settings while preserving ON,
      and USB OFF/AUTO removed and restored power to a TC64 tester and its receiver.
      The detailed integration tests below were completed on 10 October 2026.
    </div>

    <h2>What works now</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Voltage and current-limit adjustment, plus main OUTPUT ON/OFF, from one browser card.</li>
      <li>APPLY preserves the previous output state: OFF → verified settings → ON when initially ON.</li>
      <li>Main voltage, signed current and power measurements, with optional Live polling at approximately one-second intervals.</li>
      <li>USB-C controller identification, measurements and tested USB OFF/AUTO commands.</li>
      <li>Operation from a separate 12 V / 5 A source, without requiring RIDEN to power or enable P200.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The firmware accepts main setpoints of 1–30 V and 1–10 A within a 200 W limit.
      These are command bounds, not a claim that the full range has been load-tested.
      The current 60 W input source has a conservative 54 W shared budget; measured USB
      power is reserved before allowing a main-output setpoint.
    </p>
    <h2>APPLY without the manual OFF/ON sequence</h2>
    <p>
      The original firmware extension required the main output to be OFF before changing
      settings. The panel now performs that sequence automatically. It reads the live state,
      checks the source budget, switches OFF when necessary, writes and verifies both
      setpoints, then restores ON only after successful checks. A failed command or readback
      does not trigger an automatic ON retry. The output is briefly interrupted during APPLY;
      this is not uninterrupted adjustment under load.
    </p>
    <h2>Bench evidence</h2>
    <div class="fact-grid">
      <div class="fact"><strong>Main output</strong><span>5 V / 1 A ON verified by multimeter</span></div>
      <div class="fact"><strong>APPLY while ON</strong><span>5 → 6 V; P200 readback 6.034 V, no fault</span></div>
      <div class="fact"><strong>USB OFF</strong><span>TC64 dark; independent status 0 V / 0 A</span></div>
      <div class="fact"><strong>USB AUTO</strong><span>TC64 restored: 5.1 V / 1.4 A</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      The integration validation also passed 40 adapter tests, six API tests, 18 frontend
      cases and ten ARM firmware tests. The firmware update was checked by full direct
      readback after each written page. Factory regulation, protection code, calibration
      and device-specific settings were retained.
    </p>
    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img src="/images/p200-panel-20261010.png" width="464" height="1604" loading="lazy"
        alt="Labrax P200 card showing main output OFF, adjustable voltage/current, APPLY and USB-C OFF/AUTO controls" />
      <figcaption>Actual Labrax panel after the 10 October tests: main OUTPUT OFF, USB AUTO. Measurements shown here are a saved snapshot.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h2>One Pico per P200</h2>
    <p>
      The working control path is browser → local Labrax service → USB Pico bridge →
      custom GPIO link on the existing clock/data connection → P200 firmware extension.
      One Pico is sufficient for each modified P200; the extra Pico used for early bus
      investigation is not required for routine control. USB mass storage remains the
      firmware-update path rather than the runtime control link.
    </p>
    <h2>Current limits and next steps</h2>
    <p>
      USB AUTO restores factory negotiation; it does not manually select a PD voltage or
      current limit. The current custom USB command profile requires main OUTPUT OFF and
      covers 5 V receivers up to 2 A. Full device POWER control and operation with the LCD
      removed have not been implemented or validated. OUTPUT OFF switches the main
      terminals, not the entire supply.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first unit had intermittent shutdowns, including during idle tests, and was
      set aside. The working implementation was validated on the second unit, including
      a reported 30-minute stable run. This suggests a unit-specific problem in the first
      supply; its exact cause has not been established. Additional P200 channels remain
      a separate integration step.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read the <a href="https://github.com/arkadiuszpucilowski/coppertrace-site/blob/main/docs/toolkitrc-p200.md">GitHub engineering record</a>
      for the tested scope, architecture and validation results. This is a prototype record,
      not a universal firmware image for every P200 revision.
    </p>
    <h2>Initial investigation · September 2026</h2>
    <p>The following notes describe the early findings and investigation plan.</p>

    <h2>Why this supply?</h2>
    <p>
      The P200 is small, can be powered from a 24 V DC bus, provides a programmable main output,
      and also contains a useful USB-C fast-charging output. Three units could cover most
      everyday low-current bench work while a larger RIDEN supply remains available for
      occasional high-power loads.
    </p>

    <h2>Identifying the hardware</h2>
    <p>
      The unit under test is the original P200 rather than the later P200 V2. The enclosure is
      approximately 84 mm long, matching the V1 mechanical dimensions. This mattered immediately:
      V1 and V2 use different firmware packages.
    </p>

    <div class="fact-grid">
      <div class="fact">
        <strong>Model</strong>
        <span>ToolkitRC P200 V1</span>
      </div>
      <div class="fact">
        <strong>Main MCU</strong>
        <span>GD32F103CBT6 observed on PCB</span>
      </div>
      <div class="fact">
        <strong>Update interface</strong>
        <span>USB Mass Storage</span>
      </div>
      <div class="fact">
        <strong>Firmware file</strong>
        <span>ToolkitRC P200 V1.05-STN · app.upg</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>USB: useful, but not remote control</h2>
    <p>
      Connecting the P200 to Linux exposed a single USB interface: STMicroelectronics
      <code>0483:5820</code>, class 8 Mass Storage, SCSI Bulk-Only. Linux mounted a tiny FAT12
      volume labelled <code>Toolkit</code>. No CDC serial, HID or secondary vendor interface was
      exposed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The volume is therefore useful for firmware update work, but it does not provide an obvious
      documented path for normal PC control.
    </p>

    <h2>Firmware package</h2>
    <p>
      The official V1.05-STN package contains an <code>app.upg</code> image of 86,548 bytes.
      Linux identifies a Cortex-M vector table at the beginning, including an initial stack pointer
      of <code>0x200013F0</code> and a reset vector of <code>0x08009101</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Useful plaintext strings are present in the image, including output voltage/current labels,
      USB charging protocol names and a <code>START OUTPUT</code> message. However, a first-pass
      assumption that file offsets mapped directly to executable flash addresses did not survive
      testing: data at the apparent reset location did not disassemble as sensible Thumb code.
      At that stage it was recorded as an unresolved firmware-format question rather than
      forcing an interpretation.
    </p>

    <h2>PCB access changes the plan</h2>
    <p>
      Opening the unit revealed labelled test points and a debug header. The board exposes signals
      labelled for 3.3 V, reset, clock/data and several control points. The GD32F103 supports SWD,
      so a read-only debug connection was identified as an investigation path.
    </p>
    <p>
      Another group of test points appeared to expose a low-voltage serial control bus near the
      power-control section. The investigation plan was to measure the idle voltages first,
      then capture traffic while changing voltage, current limit and output state.
    </p>

    <h2>Original measurement plan</h2>
    <p>The initial plan focused on collecting evidence before modification:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Measure all labelled test-point voltages relative to ground.</li>
      <li>Capture the suspected clock/data pair with sigrok/PulseView.</li>
      <li>Record separate traces for boot, output on/off, voltage changes and current-limit changes.</li>
      <li>Verify SWD pin continuity before connecting the debugger.</li>
      <li>Keep all first SWD operations read-only; no unlock or erase operations.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The wider laboratory plan</h2>
    <p>
      The wider lab plan is a shared 24 V source feeding an independent RIDEN channel and
      two P200 channels, each with its own Pico bridge. The current validated setup uses
      one P200 with a separate 12 V / 5 A source. Completing the next channel does not require
      changing this working unit's calibration.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      Planned lab: shared 24 V DC source → RIDEN + two P200 channels → one Pico per P200 →
      Labrax Bench Control. The shared-source power budget must cover all active channels.
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
